1
00:00:00,040 --> 00:00:02,490
A cafe is missing part of its delivery.

2
00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:07,430
The supplier says, "We will credit you." Days later, a credit arrives.

3
00:00:07,440 --> 00:00:09,570
But it covers only the oat milk.

4
00:00:10,480 --> 00:00:15,520
Meet Remainder, built by Shivam Gupta: a supplier-credit desk for independent

5
00:00:15,520 --> 00:00:20,500
businesses. This demonstration uses fictional documents and real OpenAI

6
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:21,310
analysis.

7
00:00:22,300 --> 00:00:27,090
The invoice bills twelve cases of oat milk and ten cases of tomatoes.

8
00:00:27,420 --> 00:00:31,890
Every document lives in one case, with text the owner can review.

9
00:00:32,690 --> 00:00:36,910
The receiving record shows eight oat-milk cases and seven tomato cases.

10
00:00:37,420 --> 00:00:40,870
A confirmed supplier alias connects the different names for the same oat drink.

11
00:00:41,770 --> 00:00:44,650
Now the owner starts a real OpenAI request.

12
00:00:44,980 --> 00:00:46,890
The model interprets the documents.

13
00:00:47,220 --> 00:00:52,030
Remainder checks quoted evidence, units, prices, and supplier identity

14
00:00:52,240 --> 00:00:54,090
before an amount can enter a claim.

15
00:00:55,120 --> 00:00:58,750
Four missing cases at $36 make $144.

16
00:00:59,780 --> 00:01:02,610
Three tomato cases at $24 make $72.

17
00:01:03,280 --> 00:01:06,030
Together, the shortage is $216.

18
00:01:07,100 --> 00:01:08,890
Each finding links to its source.

19
00:01:09,320 --> 00:01:13,880
Nothing starts approved. The owner reviews the evidence and explicitly selects the supported

20
00:01:13,880 --> 00:01:14,850
shortages.

21
00:01:15,800 --> 00:01:18,710
Preparing the claim locks its amount and evidence.

22
00:01:19,040 --> 00:01:22,410
A later credit cannot rewrite what was originally requested.

23
00:01:22,960 --> 00:01:25,410
This claim is for $216.

24
00:01:26,620 --> 00:01:30,450
The owner downloads an evidence PDF and an email draft.

25
00:01:31,280 --> 00:01:35,420
Remainder fits the existing email workflow, and the owner decides what actually gets

26
00:01:35,420 --> 00:01:36,070
sent.

27
00:01:37,320 --> 00:01:39,090
Here is the supplier credit note.

28
00:01:39,440 --> 00:01:44,670
It names the same invoice, but credits only $144 for the oat milk.

29
00:01:45,410 --> 00:01:47,760
A second live analysis matches the credit.

30
00:01:47,760 --> 00:01:50,950
The owner inspects its source before verification.

31
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:54,970
Duplicate checks prevent the same note from being counted twice.

32
00:01:55,840 --> 00:01:59,810
$216 claimed. $144 in a verified credit note.

33
00:02:00,160 --> 00:02:03,230
$72 still outstanding. The case stays open.

34
00:02:03,840 --> 00:02:06,370
A credit note is distinct from cash received.

35
00:02:06,970 --> 00:02:12,140
The follow-up draft acknowledges the credit already issued and asks only about the

36
00:02:12,140 --> 00:02:17,530
remaining $72. That is the point: a partial credit stays partial.

37
00:02:18,780 --> 00:02:23,130
Separately verified Evorozen memory stores approved product aliases.

38
00:02:24,020 --> 00:02:28,010
Financial checks and durable balances stay in application code.

39
00:02:29,200 --> 00:02:32,570
Start with independent operators and hospitality bookkeepers.

40
00:02:33,140 --> 00:02:38,030
Measure review time and repeat use, then test $29 per location per month.

41
00:02:38,620 --> 00:02:40,310
Customer traction is not yet claimed.

42
00:02:41,260 --> 00:02:44,300
Try Remainder at remainder-desk.web.app.

43
00:02:44,300 --> 00:02:47,910
Built by Shivam Gupta for Evorozen Apex.

44
00:02:48,580 --> 00:02:50,270
Know exactly what is still outstanding.
